[TITLE] Self-monitoring of an isolated power station
[P1] On a shelf that's hard to get to. A power station feeds a dispensary, a radio relay. The micro-grid includes solar panels, lithium batteries and a small diesel generator as the road may remain closed for several days. A controller measures voltage, current, temperature and vibration every 200 milliseconds Detailed data remains in place, only a compressed summary is transmitted when the link is working Medical and communication equipment is priority
[P2] On 18 October 2025, a dust storm reduced solar output by 34% in fifteen minutes. Instead of shutting down the station, the system turned off the secondary heating, reduced some of the lights and started the backup unit. It identified three possible causes: debris on the panels, worn bearings and increasing resistance in a battery connector.
[P3] After the storm, a head-mounted camera read the license plates and alarm codes. Then a local processor compared them to the offline manual No sensitive image was sent to the cloud The software required that the entire tag remain within a time frame; it also controlled the overall sharpness and that of the upper part A sound command asked to back up if an edge was missing, and four stable images were needed The engineers then confirmed the proposed order to clean the panels, replace the bearing, then tighten the connector The intervention lasted six minutes without interrupting a critical service A reliable automation must recognize uncertainty and leave the final decision to the operator
============================================================

